Error [E0066]: this function uses the retired effect-arrow spelling
  --> tests/ui/crypto_expert_nonce_callback_bound_precedence.jet:4:39
    |
  4 | fn transform(items: [Int], f: fn(Int) =[]=> Int) => [Int] :: items.map((x) => f(x))
    |                                       ^
 Why: callable results use `:>`, and an explicit effect ceiling uses the same arrow
 Fix: write `:[Effects]>`; use `:[]>` for an explicit purity bound

Error [E0065]: This function uses the retired `::` body marker.
  --> tests/ui/crypto_expert_nonce_callback_bound_precedence.jet:4:59
    |
  4 | fn transform(items: [Int], f: fn(Int) =[]=> Int) => [Int] :: items.map((x) => f(x))
    |                                                           ^^
 Why: A one-expression function body uses `:>`; `::` binds a name, and `=` fills a slot inside a definition.
 Fix: Replace `::` with `:>`; `jet fmt` applies this fix.

Error [E0771]: this needs fn(Int) =[]=> Int, but the function value is fn(n: Int) => Int
  --> tests/ui/crypto_expert_nonce_callback_bound_precedence.jet:11:28
    |
 11 |     r :: transform([1, 2], noisy)
    |                            ^^^^^
 Why: public labels and parameter zones are part of a function's callable type
 Fix: use `fn(Int) =[]=> Int` here

Error [E0747]: this callback uses the effect `IO`, which the parameter doesn't allow
  --> tests/ui/crypto_expert_nonce_callback_bound_precedence.jet:11:28
    |
 11 |     r :: transform([1, 2], noisy)
    |                            ^^^^^
 Why: the parameter is `fn(…) =[]=>`, so the callback must be pure; `IO` is outside that bound
 Fix: pass a `fn(…) =[]=>` callback (or a lambda that uses no effects), or widen the parameter's bound
